  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EKN NAI Water Scoping and Ideation Assignment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Availability of clean and safe water is necessary for the sustainable and inclusive economic and social development and growth. Kenya is a water scarce country. At the same time, Kenya is experiencing positive economic trends, and a burgeoning youthful population. As a result the demand on water services and water management is ever increasing, hence consistently putting pressure on this scarce resource. This has led to the increasing need for innovative approaches and solutions to effectively and efficiently develop and management the water resources to meet an ever growing demand. The Netherlands has a strong track record on water development and management. Water is one of the priority themes in the Netherlands&apos; Governments foreign policy, seen to strategically reinforce the Netherlands&apos; interest areas in Trade and Investment, Migration and Refugees, and security and Stability. The Kenya Multi Annual Country Strategy (MACS) for 2026 - 2029 elaborates on strategic approach to aligne these shared interests and as such strengthen cooperation between Netherlands and Kenya.
The water scoping and ideation assignment seeks to explore and identify concrete opportunities where the Netherlands has a comparative advantage, and would contribute to transformative lasting impact in Kenya&apos;s water sector, leveraging on water as a priority theme for the Netherlands and the Netherlands interests. The scoping and ideation assignment will focus is on informing water programming under the new MACS. The activity will specifically provide findings and recommendations on drinking water and sanitation, and integrated water management. The findings and recommendations will be made having factored the extent climate change adaptation and gender dynamics as key crosscutting themes influence impact. The study is carried out with the understanding that water is a key enabler to sustainable trade and inclusive economic growth. Private sector development will be a prominent part of the study informing design and implementation of programs that will be developed out of the study.</narrative>
  </description>
